  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\n(Thursday Night - Friday Night)\n\nThere looks to be two additional rounds of widespread showers and \nthunderstorms, one Thursday night and another Friday night. For \nThursday night, the focus should shift southward, more toward \nsoutheast Missouri and southwest Illinois. This is where most \nguidance favors the stronger low-level moisture convergence. \nTraining may be a concern again with a veering low-level jet more \nparallel to the quasi-stationary front. A couple of factors though \nsuggest the flash flood threat probably will be lower: 1) \nprecipitable water values are not as high, closer to 1.8\" (near or \njust below 90th percentile), and 2) locations favored for storms \nThursday night are farther south than tonight.\n\nThe approach of a midlevel shortwave trough Friday evening and an \nincreasing low-level jet should mean another round of nocturnal \nconvection Friday night. This round probably will shift back north, \nand may occur in areas that get significant rainfall tonight into \nThursday morning. If this is indeed the case, that could mean a \nrenewed flash flood threat. However, the jet itself looks weaker \nthan either tonight or Thursday night and the rainfall does not look \nas efficient either with lower warm cloud depths and precipitable \nwater values.\n\n\n(Saturday - Monday Night)\n\nGuidance is in pretty good agreement that the quasi-stationary front \nwill move northward on Saturday as a warm front. After this front \nmoves through, the chances for any showers and storms ceases. The \npattern quickly though shifts to heat and humidity as both \ntemperatures and dewpoints increase from southwest to northeast.\nSaturday may be a bit of a transition day, with highs \napproaching 90 degrees and peak heat index values likely around \nthe century mark. Sunday and Monday look hot and humid areawide \nwith highs in the low to mid 90s and widespread 100-105 heat \nindex values. Some areas may see 105+F heat index values, which\nis magnitude heat advisory criteria.\n\n(Tuesday - Wednesday)\n\nEnsemble guidance suggests a midlevel trough will dig into the Great \nLakes early/mid next week, with the ridge shifting more toward the \ndesert southwest. There is quite a bit of spread however with the \ntrough across the Great Lakes. Roughly half of members from the LREF \nare weaker with this feature, with a surface cold front possibly not \nmaking it into our area. The other half has a stronger trough, with \na cold frontal passage across the area sometime Tuesday/Tuesday \nnight. This latter scenario would end the heat/humidity, \nwhereas the first scenario likely would continue the 100-105+ \nheat index values each afternoon.",
